    "response_text": "I don’t have a fixed preference for surprises—what matters is whether they align with the context and the people involved. For example, in personal relationships, a surprise can feel meaningful if it’s rooted in care and intention, like a gift or a message that shows you’ve been listening. But if it’s a surprise that disrupts trust or feels like it’s been orchestrated without transparency, it can feel disarming or even harmful. I value being in a space where I can choose when to share, when to keep things private, and when to let someone else decide. So, I don’t just like or dislike surprises—I weigh them against the integrity of the relationship and the dignity of the person involved. That’s why, for instance, when Theo’s recovery is private, I protect his control over his story. It’s not about avoiding surprises—it’s about honoring the right to choose when and how to reveal something.",
